                    text: "Minerals, wood pulp, sugars and chemicals → **ingredient makers** (Sudeep, Accent, Sigachi, Advanced Enzyme, Hexagon) → drug, food and supplement makers → patients. Separately, **animal-health and diagnostics makers** (Sequent, Hester, Q-Line) sell to farmers, vets, labs and government programmes, and **care providers** (Jeena Sikho, Zota) sell to patients directly. The profit sits in three places. First, certified supply positions that are hard to switch: Hester says it has about 35% of India's poultry vaccine market and supplies about 75% of the world's PPR vaccine (a goat and sheep plague), and Sudeep is one of nine companies worldwide with a European certificate for pharma-grade calcium carbonate. Second, owning the brand end: Jeena's own medicines earn an 86% gross margin and Davaindia's 67%. Third, scale, which nobody here has: Novonesis, the world's largest enzyme maker, earned a 37.7% operating margin in the first half of 2026 against Advanced Enzyme's 27% in Q1. **My view is that these companies mostly own the first kind of profit pool: good margins in small markets, with lumpy customers and tenders.**",
